#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133692 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "78e7cbb2-d207-5d5e-bf46-5c3b2686933f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8740" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{3y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+0}+3(1)^{2}{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133696 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/id #uuid "ac6e7a2c-19a3-595a-ba46-f38c653a9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8927" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d37f2a445986b2aae60603fed3bef1b480d8f049dfe17a0cf26325ac2eb167d8.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F?$\n\nI. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n\nII. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F <                    0$\n\nIV. $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F <         0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F =0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133705 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "3cbc5b4a-dde4-59b0-bc34-08c4d8776e0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9489" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3y-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{j}+(x-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j} + R\\,\\mathbf k$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}+\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x-y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((3y-2z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x-2z)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(2x-y)+6(3y-2z)-4(x-2z) \\\\  & =4x+14y-4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf { F}$ at the point $(1,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(1,2,1) & =4(1)+14(2)-4(1) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5b1d09-1da1-519d-9990-fff8f06520f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424196} {:answer/id #uuid "2181d067-a072-5d02-8afa-0c943d7e22d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424200} {:answer/id #uuid "55a8bdef-6d67-5f62-8f8a-3f474c61f4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424208} {:answer/id #uuid "af6e0ce3-9249-535e-9fb2-893259204ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424214} {:answer/id #uuid "b75001d5-d554-5e4b-9dfe-3ece61afe366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af6e0ce3-9249-535e-9fb2-893259204ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424214} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e9bf416-fabf-5d02-bf76-781ab322815f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca50a185-e281-53e0-900c-05cc2ac6e7a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-105562" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(\\pi ,1) & =2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+\\frac{\\pi }{1} \\\\  & =2(1)(1)+\\pi  \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21305c88-eeaf-5820-af29-34723d616234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-y)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424199} {:answer/id #uuid "507360f0-f40d-51b4-8d0a-a73fd33bb756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+y)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424205} {:answer/id #uuid "754db6a7-5db4-5e7b-9b14-ca330f6671e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424210} {:answer/id #uuid "88c4b453-3bda-58b8-8fbb-6a0d6916e90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424211} {:answer/id #uuid "952b9fe9-4b9b-5eac-bcfa-e800b3b1094b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88c4b453-3bda-58b8-8fbb-6a0d6916e90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424211} :answer-field/id #uuid "fadbbb52-eaa9-5960-9164-0e7ea16514af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "162e9632-ec96-53eb-bf5e-ab2bd97ae9e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-123172" :question/problem "Given that $F=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60937b06-70ea-5c5a-b823-3f7549956fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383043} {:answer/id #uuid "7b44972d-191a-5729-ae14-a89b1f4b0c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383044} {:answer/id #uuid "bc975208-2acd-5024-a13d-ca18e4d8b16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383045} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3e76e5-a29e-5d75-b27c-e2d8dfd0de01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383046} {:answer/id #uuid "8e46db48-04cb-5171-923b-8c8d70b490f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e46db48-04cb-5171-923b-8c8d70b490f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383047} :answer-field/id #uuid "81a2e1fd-a1e3-5cc4-a1da-9e8f0ebbe408" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c8a3eea-616a-5c49-bb08-64624b7ee7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-123192" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+x{y}^{2}z\\,j+xy{z}^{2}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xyz+2xyz+2xyz \\\\  & =6xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,2,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,2,2) & =6(1)(2)(2) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193ddeed-a793-53c6-ba1e-92e68b0f73f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424198} {:answer/id #uuid "4849233c-e677-553e-b525-8ce1ed84e89f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424203} {:answer/id #uuid "6e36ebd6-f283-5b52-b5a9-f667a2f4b389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424209} {:answer/id #uuid "cd60ac0d-9b78-5c69-bd7b-06d4c14bdb66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424219} {:answer/id #uuid "f47d0d8f-c2bd-587b-8969-e8a2b85184c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4849233c-e677-553e-b525-8ce1ed84e89f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424203} :answer-field/id #uuid "3452daaf-b0f7-51b4-a94c-d00ee7b3816f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df216990-7a71-5841-8a5b-955dc6939d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123196" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+(y+z)^{2}\\,j+(x+z)^{2}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x+y)+2(y+z)+2(x+z) \\\\  & =4(x+y+z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,1,-1) & =4((-2)+(1)+(-1)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51328c89-8cdd-5ec3-96c7-541a1dd6496d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424206} {:answer/id #uuid "53e20d90-4e62-516b-ae3d-39f75e6f0b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424207} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ea2773-828b-51a5-b56b-8c1ce5f3d0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424215} {:answer/id #uuid "c14c9ebe-eb84-5d9c-8dd2-5d0a1d71c120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424217} {:answer/id #uuid "cd47d87e-fdba-55d2-b443-02e11d1f6d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1ea2773-828b-51a5-b56b-8c1ce5f3d0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424215} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8def5ae-54bc-5cde-af27-af1ce38db335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5777673f-cba1-5775-ae01-f18d4531f239" :question/math-academy-id "q-131195" :question/problem "![](images/39/39dfec02c787141d8a728381f4093dfe9cbf3fdbf996177ba6f24d973015dc5f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f86bf7-5c17-5cb6-8c9a-25e3a30c5a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424197} {:answer/id #uuid "26003085-8363-5ee2-8473-69f3ba602654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424201} {:answer/id #uuid "4452f92a-63c8-5376-8e98-947775e29e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424202} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd6eba8-c859-5a8d-b522-f2c3b0ee5a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424204} {:answer/id #uuid "8aff9788-bb7e-5bb2-9074-85e6460dae3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18f86bf7-5c17-5cb6-8c9a-25e3a30c5a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424197} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd542c0-4d3b-584d-9d7b-3c0d0b5750ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9278ec55-4908-5c1c-8a74-640611473537" :question/math-academy-id "q-131196" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bf8de7e8719545b3d8abf5dc9fb4bc0c5f71034dba7186b7d9822d9e8be044d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $x$-components decrease as we move horizontally towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
